-- title:
--   Regularity package for derivative words of centre-finite translates
-- statement:
--   Fix a character $\omega$ of the idele class group, that is a monoid homomorphism $(\mathbb A_{\mathbb Q})^\times \to \mathbb C^\times$ with $\lVert\omega(z)\rVert = 1$ for all $z$, and a function $f$ on $\mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb A_{\mathbb Q})$ which is continuous, invariant under left translation by the image of $\mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb Q)$, transforms by $\omega$ under the central scalars, is of moderate growth in the sense that $\lVert f(g)\rVert \le C\,\mathrm{gauge}_3(g)^N$ for some $C$ and $N$, satisfies the vanishing of the two iterated integrals of $f$ over the two-dimensional unipotent radicals attached to the maximal parabolics $P_{21}$ and $P_{12}$ (the measure being the Haar measure of the adeles conditioned on the adelic box), is smooth in the archimedean matrix variable, and is orthogonally finite: there is a finite set $s$ of functions such that for every $k$ trivial at all finite places whose archimedean component $\kappa$ satisfies $\kappa^{\mathsf T}\kappa = 1$, the translate $g \mapsto f(gk)$ lies in the $\mathbb C$-span of $s$. Let $c : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to \mathbb C$ and $t : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to \mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb A_{\mathbb Q})$ with each $t_i$ trivial at infinity, and assume $v = \sum_i c_i f(\cdot\, t_i)$ is centre-finite, i.e. annihilated by a monic polynomial in each of the three operators $\mathrm{casimir}_1, \mathrm{casimir}_2, \mathrm{casimir}_3$ assembled from the nine archimedean derivatives $\mathrm{archDeriv}\ i\ j$. Let $u$ lie in the $\mathbb C$-span of the set of functions of the form $D\bigl(g \mapsto \sum_i c_i f(g h t_i)\bigr)$, where $h$ runs over $\mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb A_{\mathbb Q})$ and $D$ over the words in the nine archimedean derivatives. Further data are given: natural numbers $m, J$, an injective $e : \mathrm{Fin}\,m \to \mathbb C$, coefficient functions $a_{ij}(y_2, k)$ continuous on $\{y_2 > 0\}$, an exponent $\tau > 1/2$, the hypothesis that on each compact set of $k$ and each band $b^{-1} \le y_2 \le b$ the Whittaker integral of $u$ against the standard additive character $\psi_{\mathbb Q}$, evaluated at $\mathrm{diag}(y_1y_2, y_2, 1)\,k$, differs from $\sum_{i,j} a_{ij}(y_2,k)\, y_1^{e_i} (\log y_1)^j$ by $O(y_1^{\tau})$ for $0 < y_1 \le 1$, and an index $i_0$ with $\mathrm{Re}\,e_{i_0} = 1/2$. The conclusion is that $u$ is continuous, invariant under left translation by $\mathrm{GL}_3(\mathbb Q)$, transforms by $\omega$ under central scalars, is of moderate growth, is cuspidal along $P_{21}$ and along $P_{12}$ for the same measure data, is smooth in the archimedean variable, and every word in the nine archimedean derivatives applied to $u$ is continuous.
--
--   This is the assembly step which transfers the standard regularity properties of a cuspidal automorphic form on $\mathrm{GL}_3$ with unitary central character to an arbitrary vector of the module generated by archimedean derivative words of right translates of a centre-finite combination of translates; each property is stable under right translation, under the nine archimedean derivatives on smooth vectors, and under finite linear combinations. It feeds the construction of the smoothing submodule and the analysis of leading coefficients of Whittaker expansions with exponent of real part $1/2$.
